  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA better potential for significant rainfall arrives Sunday night \nlasting into Tuesday. A developing low pressure system off the \nRockies will push eastward into Nebraska and South Dakota bringing \nwidespread rain showers and even some isolated thunderstorms to the \nregion. While QPF amounts are still a little uncertain, confidence \nis high that most locations, especially north of Highway 2, will see \nat least a half inch of rainfall. Currently, the best instability \nwill remain off to the east and south keeping the severe risk low, \nhowever, QPF could exceed over an inch under some of the heavier \nshowers or thunderstorms. While these rainfall totals are \nsignificant, the longer duration of this system should keep any \nflooding concerns at bay. Still, will be monitoring the rainfall \ntotals and flooding potential closely as the event unfolds. Will \nalso need to monitor the precipitation type as the system begins to \npush out Monday night and Tuesday morning. Colder air filtering in \nbehind the departing low may result in a brief change over to snow \nacross the higher elevations of the Pine Ridge. At this time, \nconfidence remains too low to put in the forecast, but if the colder \nair can push in quickly, it will be possible to see a few wet \nsnowflakes early Tuesday morning. Regardless if this happens, not \nexpecting any accumulations. \n\nDrier weather returns for mid-week through the end of the week as \nupper level ridging builds back over the Great Plains. Despite the \nquieter conditions, temperatures will remain slightly below normals \nWednesday through Friday. Highs will remain in the low to mid 50s \nacross the area (normals in the upper 50s to low 60s). Lows will \ndrop into the low to mid 30s, which coincidentally is a few degrees \nabove normals in the upper 20s.",
